<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cosmas Indicopleustes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">6th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cosmas Indicopleustes, 'the India-voyager', was a sixth-century merchant from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> who travelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean-trade">Indian Ocean trade</a> routes and later became a monk. His Christian Topography, written around 550, argued that the world was flat and shaped like the Tabernacle, but it preserves valuable descriptions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksum</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> and the trade of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>, including the wide acceptance of the Byzantine gold coin.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cosmas-indicopleustes.jpg" alt="Cosmas Indicopleustes" width="320" height="251" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cosmas Indicopleustes, 6th century · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:WorldMapCosmasIndicopleustes.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
